'''Kathleen Mary Hochul'''{{Efn|/ˈhoʊkəl/ HOH-kəl}}'''{{efn|While several sources list her name as "Kathleen Courtney Hochul", her legal name is "Kathleen Mary Hochul". "Mary" is her middle name, while "Courtney" is her maiden name. She uses "Kathleen Courtney Hochul" in professional contexts, but it is not her legal name.}}'''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Former Rep. Kathy Hochul - D New York, 26th, Defeated - 57th Governor of New York; 77th Lieutenant Governor of New York - Biography {{!}} LegiStorm |url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/169191/Kathleen_Mary_Hochul.html |access-date=2026-07-29 |website=www.legistorm.com |language=en}}</ref> ({{née|'''Courtney'''}}; born August 27, 1958)<ref>{{congbio|inline = yes|id = H001062|accessdate = January 17, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2021-08-11 |title=Kathy Hochul: New York's next governor influenced by Irish roots |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-58174022 |access-date=2026-07-06 |language=en-GB}}</ref> is an American politician and lawyer who has served since 2021 as the 57th [[governor of New York]]. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she is New York's [[List of female governors in the United States|first female]] governor.

Born in [[Buffalo, New York]], Hochul graduated from [[Syracuse University]] in 1980 and the [[Columbus School of Law|Catholic University of America Columbus School of Law]] in 1984. After serving on the town board of [[Hamburg, New York|Hamburg]] and as deputy [[Erie County, New York|Erie County]] clerk, Hochul was appointed Erie County clerk in 2007. She was elected to a full term as Erie County clerk in 2007 and reelected in 2010. In May 2011, Hochul won a four-candidate [[2011 New York's 26th congressional district special election|special election]] for New York's 26th congressional district and became the first Democrat to represent the district in 40 years. She served as a [[U.S. representative]] from 2011 to 2013 and was narrowly defeated for reelection in [[2012 United States House of Representatives elections in New York#District 27|2012]] by Republican [[Chris Collins (New York politician)|Chris Collins]] following [[2010 United States redistricting cycle|redistricting]].

In the [[2014 New York gubernatorial election]], incumbent Governor [[Andrew Cuomo]] selected Hochul as his running mate; after they won the election, Hochul was inaugurated as lieutenant governor. Cuomo and Hochul were reelected in [[2018 New York gubernatorial election|2018]]. Hochul took office as governor of New York on August 24, 2021, after Cuomo resigned amid [[Andrew Cuomo sexual harassment allegations|allegations of sexual harassment]]. She won a full term in [[2022 New York gubernatorial election|2022]] against Republican nominee and U.S. Representative [[Lee Zeldin]] in the narrowest New York gubernatorial election since [[1994 New York gubernatorial election|1994]].

==Early life and education==
Kathleen Mary Courtney was born in [[Buffalo, New York]], the second of the six children of John Patrick "Jack" Courtney, a clerical worker, and Patricia Ann "Pat" Courtney (née Rochford), a [[homemaker]].<ref name=nytimes_20110529/><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://buffalonews.com/news/local/patricia-ann-courtney-active-in-community-mother-of-former-congresswoman/article_0b3fec93-9fd7-505a-a6bb-7a3186196638.html|title=Patricia Ann Courtney, active in community, mother of former congresswoman|website=[[The Buffalo News]]|date=March 25, 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Family tree of Kathy Hochul |url=https://en.geneastar.org/genealogy/courtneykat/kathy-hochul |access-date=2026-07-06 |website=Geneanet |language=en}}</ref> Her family is [[Irish Catholic]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://kathyhochul.com/bio/|title=Bio|website=Kathy Hochul|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302075700/https://kathyhochul.com/bio/|archive-date=March 2, 2021}}</ref> The family struggled financially during Hochul's early years and for a time lived in a trailer near a steel plant.<ref name="nytimes_20110529"/> By the time Hochul was in college, her father was working for the [[information technology]] company he later headed.<ref name="nytimes_20110529"/> 

Hochul graduated from [[Hamburg High School (Hamburg, New York)|Hamburg High School]] in 1976.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hamburg High School staff look back at Hochul's journey |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/buffalo/news/2021/08/11/hamburg-high-school-looks-back-at-kathy-hochul-s-journey |access-date=2025-07-28 |website=Spectrum News |language=en}}</ref> She was politically active during her college years at [[Syracuse University]], becoming a student government vice president. She led a boycott of the student bookstore over high prices and an unsuccessful effort to name the university stadium after alumnus [[Ernie Davis]], a star [[running back]] who died of cancer before he could join the [[National Football League]].<ref name=nytimes_20110529/><ref name="roots in Syracuse"/> Hochul also lobbied the university to [[Disinvestment from South Africa|divest from apartheid South Africa]].<ref name="roots in Syracuse" /> The university divested in November 1985, more than five years after she graduated.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Marsh |first=Andrea C. |date=Summer 1994 |title=Answering A Call – SU lifts its ban on South African investment |url=https://surface.syr.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1470&context=sumagazine |access-date=July 28, 2025 |website=SURFACE at Syracuse University}}</ref> In the spring of 1979, the student newspaper ''[[The Daily Orange]]'' awarded her an "A", citing the campus changes as evidence for the grade.<ref name="roots in Syracuse"/>

Hochul received a [[Bachelor of Arts]] with a major in [[political science]] from the [[Maxwell School of Citizenship and Public Affairs]] at Syracuse University in 1980. She enrolled at the [[Columbus School of Law]] of the [[Catholic University of America]] in [[Washington, D.C.]], and received a [[Juris Doctor]] in 1984.<ref name=nytimes_20110529/><ref>{{cite news |title=Alumna Kathleen Hochul '80 Becomes First Female Governor of New York State |url=https://news.syr.edu/blog/2021/08/25/alumna-kathleen-hochul-80-becomes-first-female-governor-of-new-york-state/ |access-date=August 25, 2021 |work=SU News |date=August 25, 2021}}</ref><ref name="profile going"/>

==Early career==
After graduation from law school, Hochul began working for a Washington, D.C., law firm, but she found the work unsatisfying.<ref name=nytimes_20110529/> She then worked as legal counsel and legislative assistant to U.S. Representative [[John LaFalce]] and U.S. Senator [[Daniel Moynihan]], and also for the [[New York State Assembly]], before seeking elected office.<ref name="EC Clerk appoints"/><ref name="defines herself"/>

Hochul became involved in local issues as a supporter of small businesses facing competition from [[Walmart]] stores<ref name="defines herself"/> and, in the process, caught the attention of local Democratic leaders.<ref>{{cite news|last=Mahoney|first=Bill|date=August 22, 2021|title=Hamburg's 'energizer bunny': Hochul's time in town government|work=[[Politico]]|url=https://www.politico.com/states/new-york/albany/story/2021/08/22/hamburgs-energizer-bunny-hochuls-time-in-town-government-1390296|url-status=live|access-date=August 24, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210823001824/https://www.politico.com/states/new-york/albany/story/2021/08/22/hamburgs-energizer-bunny-hochuls-time-in-town-government-1390296|archive-date=August 23, 2021}}</ref> On January 3, 1994, the [[Hamburg, New York#Government|Hamburg Town Board]] voted to appoint her to the vacant seat on the board caused by Patrick H. Hoak's resignation to become town supervisor.<ref>{{cite web|last=McCarthy|first=Molly|date=January 4, 1994|title=Hochul named to Hamburg Town Board, vacancy created by Hoak's election as supervisor filled|url=https://buffalonews.com/news/hochul-named-to-hamburg-town-board-vacancy-created-by-hoaks-election-as-supervisor-filled/article_1642781b-9622-544a-a93e-adb4216dbc38.html|access-date=August 24, 2021|website=[[The Buffalo News]]|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=January 3, 1994|title=Hamburg Town Board meeting (minutes)|url=https://www.townofhamburgny.gov/wp-content/uploads/01031994.pdf|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210824053419/https://www.townofhamburgny.com/wp-content/uploads/01031994.pdf|archive-date=August 24, 2021|access-date=August 24, 2021|website=[[Hamburg, New York|Town of Hamburg, New York]]}}</ref> She was elected to a full term in November 1994, on the Democratic and [[Conservative Party of New York State|Conservative]] lines,<ref name="how should"/><ref name="Jann retakes"/> and was reelected in 1998, 2002, and 2006. She resigned on April 10, 2007,<ref name = "becomes clerk">{{cite web|last=McCarthy|first=Robert|date=April 10, 2007|title=Hochul appointed to succeed Swarts, job means pay cut for new county clerk|url=https://buffalonews.com/news/hochul-appointed-to-succeed-swarts-job-means-pay-cut-for-new-county-clerk/article_32ee99ca-a958-5969-bf48-43e6f7d18b1b.html|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210822194557/https://buffalonews.com/news/hochul-appointed-to-succeed-swarts-job-means-pay-cut-for-new-county-clerk/article_32ee99ca-a958-5969-bf48-43e6f7d18b1b.html|archive-date=August 22, 2021|access-date=June 4, 2021|website=[[The Buffalo News]]|language=en}}</ref> and was succeeded by former state assemblymember Richard A. Smith.<ref>{{cite web|date=April 23, 2007|title=Hamburg Town Board meeting (minutes)|url=https://www.townofhamburgny.com/wp-content/uploads/042307tb.pdf|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210824053547/https://www.townofhamburgny.com/wp-content/uploads/042307tb.pdf|archive-date=August 24, 2021|access-date=August 24, 2021|website=[[Hamburg, New York|Town of Hamburg, New York]]}}</ref> While on the board, she led efforts to remove [[toll booth]]s from parts of the [[New York State Thruway]] system.<ref name="differ on more"/><ref name="to air"/>

In May 2003, [[Erie County, New York|Erie County]] Clerk [[David Swarts]] appointed Hochul as his deputy.<ref name="EC Clerk appoints"/><ref name="how should"/> Governor [[Eliot Spitzer]] named Swarts to his administration in January 2007 and appointed Hochul to succeed Swarts as county clerk in April 2007.<ref name = "becomes clerk"/> In an intervention that raised her statewide profile, Hochul opposed Spitzer's proposal to allow undocumented immigrants to obtain driver's licenses without producing [[Social Security (United States)|Social Security]] cards. She said that if the proposal went into effect, she would seek to have any such applicants arrested.<ref name="license debate"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.politico.com/states/new-york/city-hall/story/2021/03/02/meet-the-woman-ready-to-come-out-of-cuomos-shadow-1366427|title=Meet the woman ready to come out of Cuomo's shadow |date=March 2, 2021|access-date=June 4, 2021|work=[[Politico]]|first1=Bill|last1=Mahoney|first2=Marie J.|last2=French}}</ref> On November 6, 2007, Hochul was elected to fill the remainder of Swarts's term.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.btpm.org/2007-11-07/hochul-elected-county-clerk-other-races-of-note|title=Hochul Elected County Clerk, Other Races of Note|date=November 7, 2007|website=Buffalo Toronto Public Media}}</ref><ref name=buffalo_news_20101103/> On November 14, 2007, Spitzer abandoned his effort to allow undocumented immigrants to obtain driver's licenses.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/news/11250-spitzer-abandons-plan-to-give-illegals-drivers-licenses/|title=Spitzer abandons plan to give illegals driver's licenses|website=thehill.com|last=Marre |first=Klaus |date=November 14, 2007}}</ref>

In 2010, New York Democrats proposed mandating that all New York drivers obtain new license plates. The proposal was expected to bring the state government $129 million in revenue. Hochul opposed the proposal, and it was withdrawn.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lockportjournal.com/news/local_news/kathy-hochul-defines-herself/article_e25c4430-e4ff-5abe-9cf6-cda6deadea11.html|title=Kathy Hochul defines herself|last=Miles |first=Joyce|date=May 20, 2011|website=Lockport Union-Sun & Journal}}</ref>

In November 2010, Hochul ran for reelection on four [[Qualified New York parties|ballot lines]] ([[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]], [[Conservative Party of New York|Conservative]], [[Independence Party of New York|Independence]], and [[Working Families Party|Working Families]]), defeating Republican Clifton Bergfeld with 80% of the vote.<ref name="defines herself"/><ref name="2010 elections"/>

After Hochul's departure as county clerk, newly elected County Clerk Chris Jacobs discovered a backlog of mail and later said that $792,571 in checks were found in it.<ref name="Gee">Gee, Denise Jewell (May 4, 2012). "[https://buffalonews.com/2012/05/04/county-clerk-says-office-backlog-should-be-eliminated-within-weeks/ County clerk says office backlog should be eliminated within weeks]". ''[[The Buffalo News]]''. buffalonews.com. Retrieved September 10, 2018.</ref>

==U.S. House of Representatives (2011–2013)==
===Elections===
====2011 special====
{{Main|2011 New York's 26th congressional district special election}}

[[File:President Barack Obama walks along the Colonnade of the White House with newly-elected Rep. Kathy Hochul, D-N.Y.jpg|thumb|Hochul with President [[Barack Obama]], following her 2011 election to represent New York's 26th district]]
Hochul ran in the May 24, 2011, [[New York's 26th congressional district special election, 2011|special election]] to fill the seat in [[New York's 26th congressional district]] left vacant by the resignation of [[Chris Lee (New York politician)|Chris Lee]]. She was the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]] and [[Working Families Party]] nominee.<ref name=the_hill_20110319/> Hochul's residence in [[Hamburg, New York|Hamburg]], just outside the 26th district, became an issue during her campaign, though it did not disqualify her from seeking the seat.<ref name="nyt524"/> One month after her victory, she moved into the district.<ref name="nyt524"/><ref name="moves"/>

The [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] and [[Conservative Party of New York|Conservative Party]] nominee, [[New York State Assembly|State Assemblymember]] [[Jane Corwin]], was at first strongly favored to win in the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican-leaning]] district, which had sent a Republican to Congress for the previous four decades.<ref name=nytimes_20110529/><ref name="why ny-26"/><ref name="test market"/> A late April poll showed Corwin leading Hochul by 36% to 31%; [[Tea Party movement|Tea Party]] candidate [[Jack Davis (industrialist)|Jack Davis]] trailed at 23%.<ref name="real race"/> An early May poll gave Hochul a lead of 35% to 31%,<ref name="fresh aim"/> and shortly thereafter the nonpartisan ''[[Rothenberg Political Report]]'' called the race a toss-up.<ref name="Rothenberg"/> Additional polling in the days immediately before the election had Hochul leading by four- and six-point margins.<ref name="siena may 21"/><ref name="ppp may 22"/>

A ''[[Washington Post]]'' article noted that in the face of a possible Hochul victory, there was already a "full blown spin war" about the meaning of the result. The article said that Democrats viewed the close race as a result of Republicans' budget proposal "[[The Path to Prosperity]]", and, in particular, their proposal for [[Medicare (United States)|Medicare]] reform. Republicans viewed it as the result of Davis's third-party candidacy.<ref name="Better">{{cite news|title=Is Kathy Hochul just a better candidate?|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/post/is-kathy-hochul-just-a-better-candidate/2011/05/23/AFqVvz9G_blog.html |last=Weiner |first=Rachel |date=May 23, 2011 |newspaper=Washington Post|access-date=September 10, 2018}}</ref>

The campaign featured a number of negative television ads. [[FactCheck]] accused both sides of "taking liberties with the facts". In particular, FactCheck criticized the Democrats' ads for claiming that Corwin would "essentially end Medicare", even though the plan left Medicare intact for current beneficiaries.<ref name="test market"/> The organization also faulted the Republicans for ads portraying Hochul as a puppet of former [[Speaker of the United States House of Representatives|Speaker]] [[Nancy Pelosi]], and for claiming that Hochul planned to cut [[Social Security (United States)|Social Security]] and [[Medicare (United States)|Medicare]] benefits.<ref name="test market"/>

Hochul was endorsed by [[EMILY's List]], a [[political action committee]] that supports Democratic women candidates who support [[abortion rights]].<ref name="emily's list"/><ref name="emily's list1"/> She was the fifth largest recipient of EMILY's List funds in 2011, receiving more than $27,000 in bundled donations.<ref name="emily's list1"/> The [[Democrat and Chronicle]] endorsed Hochul "for her tenacity and independence",<ref name="is best"/> while ''[[The Buffalo News]]'' endorsed her for her positions on preserving Medicare and her record of streamlining government.<ref name="bn endorsement"/>

Hochul defeated Corwin 47% to 42%, with Davis receiving 9% and [[Green Party of New York|Green Party]] candidate [[Ian Murphy (writer)|Ian Murphy]] 1%.<ref name="auto1">{{cite web|url=https://www.elections.ny.gov/NYSBOE/elections/2011/Special/26CDSpecialVoteResults.pdf|title=2011 special election results, U.S. House of Representatives, New York's 26th congressional district|website=Elections.NY.gov|access-date=December 31, 2021|archive-date=May 27, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220527024415/https://www.elections.ny.gov/NYSBOE/elections/2011/Special/26CDSpecialVoteResults.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>

====2012====
{{See also|2012 United States House of Representatives elections in New York#District 27}}
Before the 2012 election, Hochul's district was renumbered the 27th during the redistricting process. The district was redrawn in a manner that caused it to be more heavily Republican.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lockportjournal.com/news/local_news/collins-to-run-for-ny-27/article_bb4ae145-2946-5002-8d86-0f08392f6276.html|title=Collins to run for NY-27|first=Jim |last=Krencik|website=Lockport Union-Sun & Journal|date=March 26, 2012 }}</ref> Hochul was endorsed by the [[Political Victory Fund|NRA Political Victory Fund]].<ref name="NRA">{{cite web |title=Hochul endorsed by National Rifle Association |url=https://www.wnypapers.com/news/article/current/2012/10/03/108013/hochul-endorsed-by-national-rifle-association |website=wnypapers.com |access-date=20 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210309043525/https://www.wnypapers.com/news/article/current/2012/10/03/108013/hochul-endorsed-by-national-rifle-association |archive-date=9 March 2021 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Buffalonews"/> She lost to Republican [[Chris Collins (New York politician)|Chris Collins]], 51% to 49%.<ref name="Buffalonews">{{cite news|title=Cuomo adds Hochul to ticket to provide upstate support|url=http://www.buffalonews.com/city-region/buffalo-politics/cuomo-adds-hochul-to-ticket-to-provide-upstate-support-20140521|newspaper=Buffalo News|date=May 21, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306151936/http://www.buffalonews.com/city-region/buffalo-politics/cuomo-adds-hochul-to-ticket-to-provide-upstate-support-20140521|archive-date=March 6, 2016}}</ref>

===Tenure===
[[File:Kathy Hochul official portrait.jpg|thumb|upright|Hochul during the [[112th United States Congress|112th Congress]], 2011]]

In Hochul's first few weeks in office, she co-sponsored bills with [[Brian Higgins]] to streamline the passport acquisition process.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Confessore |first1=Nicholas |last2=Vitello |first2=Paul |date=2007-11-04 |title=License Issue Figures Large in Local Races |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2007/11/04/nyregion/04license.html |access-date=2023-07-03 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> She also met with [[President of the United States|then-President]] [[Barack Obama]] about the economy and job creation and introduced a motion to restore the Republican cuts to the [[Commodity Futures Trading Commission]]. She looked for ways to reduce the federal budget deficit and expressed support for reducing [[Medicaid]] spending as long as the reductions would not be achieved in the form of [[block grant]]s offered to states, as proposed in the Republican budget blueprint. She also spoke with Obama about ending tax breaks for oil companies and protecting small businesses.<ref name=Remember>{{cite web |last=Cogan |first=Marin |date=June 22, 2011 |title=Remember Kathy Hochul?|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2011/06/remember-kathy-hochul-057554 |website=Politico|access-date=September 10, 2018}}</ref>

While campaigning for Congress, Hochul called herself an "[[Independent politician|independent]] [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrat]]".<ref name="defines herself"/> In an interview with the ''[[Lockport Union-Sun & Journal]]'', she cited as examples of her independence her opposition to then-Governor [[Eliot Spitzer]]'s driver's license program for undocumented immigrants and her opposition to then-Governor [[David Paterson]]'s 2010 proposal to raise revenue by requiring all vehicle owners to buy new license plates.<ref name="defines herself"/>

Hochul was one of 17 Democrats to join Republicans in supporting a resolution finding [[United States Attorney General]] [[Eric Holder]] in contempt of Congress relating to the [[ATF gunwalking scandal]], a vote on which the NRA, which supported the resolution, announced it would be scoring lawmakers.<ref>{{cite news|title= In 'fast and furious' session, upstate Democrats Hochul and Owens vote to hold Holder in contempt |url= https://www.politico.com/states/new-york/city-hall/story/2012/06/in-fast-and-furious-session-upstate-democrats-hochul-and-owens-vote-to-hold-holder-in-contempt-000000 |last=Pillifant |first=Reid |website=Politico|date=June 28, 2012 |access-date=September 10, 2018}}</ref> Later in 2012, Hochul "trumpeted" her endorsement by the NRA and noted that she was just one of two [[New York (state)|New York]] [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] to receive its support.<ref>{{cite news |last=Benjamin |first=Liz |title=NRA Backs Hochul |url=http://www.nystateofpolitics.com/2012/10/nra-backs-hochul/ |newspaper=State of Politics. nystateofpolitics.com |date=October 3, 2012 |access-date=September 10, 2018 |archive-date=August 10, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190810063020/https://www.nystateofpolitics.com/2012/10/nra-backs-hochul/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

