== Lieutenant governor of New York (2015–2021) ==

===Elections===

====2014====
{{See also|2014 New York gubernatorial election}}
[[File:HochulMorelleLaborDayParade2014.JPG|thumb|Hochul with assembly majority leader [[Joseph Morelle]] at the 2014 Labor Day parade in [[Rochester, New York]]]]
In 2014, [[Robert Duffy (politician)|Robert Duffy]] announced that he would not run [[New York gubernatorial election, 2014|for reelection]] as lieutenant governor.<ref>{{cite news|last=Silverman|first=Justin Rocket|date=May 12, 2014|title=Robert Duffy will not seek reelection as lieutenant governor, so are you up for it?|newspaper=New York Daily News|url=http://www.nydailynews.com/news/politics/lieutenant-governor-article-1.1786535}}</ref> Incumbent Governor Andrew Cuomo was running for a second term. After Duffy's announcement, Cuomo named Hochul—who had been working as a government relations executive for [[M&T Bank]]—as his choice for lieutenant governor.<ref>{{cite news|last=Kaplan|first=Thomas|date=May 21, 2014|title=Cuomo Picks Ex-Congresswoman as Running Mate: Kathy Hochul to Be Democratic Candidate for Lieutenant Governor in New York|work=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/22/nyregion/kathy-hochul-to-be-democratic-candidate-for-lieutenant-governor-in-new-york.html?_r=0}}</ref> On May 22, 2014, the delegates to the state Democratic convention formally endorsed Hochul for lieutenant governor.<ref>{{cite news|last=Newberg|first=Rich|date=May 22, 2014|title=Hochul addresses Democrats at convention|newspaper=WIVB-TV|location=Buffalo, New York|url=http://wivb.com/2014/05/22/hochul-addresses-democrats-at-convention/|access-date=November 25, 2014|archive-date=May 28, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160528234252/http://wivb.com/2014/05/22/hochul-addresses-democrats-at-convention/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In September, Cuomo and Hochul won their Democratic primary elections, with Hochul defeating Timothy Wu.<ref>{{cite news|last=Kaplan|first=Thomas|date=September 9, 2014|title=Cuomo Defeats Teachout, Liberal Rival, in the Democratic Primary|newspaper=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/10/nyregion/cuomo-and-hochul-win-new-york-primary.html}}</ref> They were also the Working Families Party nominees.<ref>{{cite news|title=Working Families Party: Stories Working Families Party urges reluctant members to vote on its line for Cuomo, and Cuomo's new women's political party serves multiple purposes|newspaper=WRVO Public Media|url=http://wrvo.org/term/working-families-party|access-date=November 25, 2014}}</ref> (In New York, candidates for governor and lieutenant governor are nominated separately, but run as a ticket in the general election.)<ref>{{cite news|last=Martin|first=Longman|date=May 29, 2014|title=As Goes New York, So Goes the Nation|newspaper=Washington Monthly|url=http://www.washingtonmonthly.com/political-animal-a/2014_05/as_goes_new_york_so_goes_the_n050543.php|access-date=November 25, 2014|archive-date=December 3, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141203194006/http://www.washingtonmonthly.com/political-animal-a/2014_05/as_goes_new_york_so_goes_the_n050543.php|url-status=dead}}</ref> In November, the Cuomo/Hochul ticket won the general election.<ref>{{cite news|date=November 4, 2014|title=Gov. Cuomo Wins 2nd Term, Becomes 1st Democratic Gov. Since His Father To Win Re-Election In NY|newspaper=CBS New York|url=http://newyork.cbslocal.com/2014/11/04/cuomo-astorino-face-off-in-race-for-governor/}}</ref> Hochul was sworn in as lieutenant governor on January 1, 2015.<ref>{{cite web|date=November 15, 2014|title=2014 General Election Results|url=http://www.nga.org/cms/ElectionResults|website=National Governors Association}}</ref>

====2018====
{{See also|2018 New York gubernatorial election}}

In the 2018 Democratic primary for lieutenant governor of New York, Hochul defeated [[Jumaane Williams]], a member of the [[New York City Council]], 53.3%–46.7%.<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Almukhtar|first1=Sarah|last2=Bloch|first2=Matthew|last3=Lee|first3=Jasmine C.|date=September 13, 2018|title=New York Primary Election Results (Published 2018)|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/09/13/us/elections/results-new-york-primary-elections.html,%20https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2018/09/13/us/elections/results-new-york-primary-elections.html|access-date=October 9, 2020|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/09/13/nyregion/kathy-hochul-lieutenant-governor-jumaane-williams.html|title=Kathy Hochul beats back challenge from Jumaane Williams in lieutenant governor race|last=Foderaro|first=Lisa W.|date=September 13, 2018|work=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=September 13, 2021|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> In the November 6 general election, the Cuomo-Hochul ticket defeated the Republican ticket of [[Marc Molinaro]] and Julie Killian, 59.6%–36.2%.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.elections.ny.gov/NYSBOE/elections/2018/general/2018Governor.pdf |title=Certified Results from the November 6, 2018 General Election for Governor and Lt. Governor |publisher=New York State Board of Elections |access-date=December 31, 2021 |archive-date=January 17, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210117011943/https://www.elections.ny.gov/NYSBOE/elections/2018/general/2018Governor.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Tenure===
Cuomo tasked Hochul with chairing the 10 regional economic development councils that were the centerpiece of his economic development plan.<ref>{{cite web|date=November 4, 2014|title=Hochul makes history for herself and for Western New York|url=http://buffalonews.com/2014/11/04/hochul-makes-history-for-herself-and-for-western-new-york/|access-date=February 16, 2018|website=The Buffalo News|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170331031328/http://buffalonews.com/2014/11/04/hochul-makes-history-for-herself-and-for-western-new-york/ |archive-date=March 31, 2017}}</ref> He also appointed her to chair the Task Force on Heroin and Opioid Abuse and Addiction. In this capacity, she convened eight outreach sessions across New York State to hear from experts and community members in search of answers to the heroin crisis and worked to develop a comprehensive strategy for New York.<ref>{{cite web|title=Kathy Hochul to lead 23-member anti-heroin task force|website=[[New York Daily News]]|date=May 11, 2016 |url=http://www.nydailynews.com/news/politics/kathy-hochul-lead-23-member-anti-heroin-task-force-article-1.2632518|access-date=February 16, 2018}}</ref>

Hochul spearheaded Cuomo's "Enough is Enough" campaign to combat sexual assault on college campuses beginning in 2015, hosting and attending more than 25 events.<ref>{{cite web|last=Swann|first=Sara|date=September 30, 2015|title=Lt. Gov. Kathy Hochul gives 'Enough is Enough' presentation at SU|url=http://dailyorange.com/2015/09/lt-gov-kathy-hochul-gives-enough-is-enough-presentation-at-su/|access-date=February 16, 2018|work=The Daily Orange}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Willis|first=Jasmine|date=September 1, 2016|title='Enough is Enough' on campus|url=http://www.dansvilleonline.com/news/20160901/enough-is-enough-on-campus|access-date=February 16, 2018|work=Genesee Country Express}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Lieutenant Governor Kathy Hochul Brings 'Enough is Enough' Message to College Campuses – SUNY Geneseo|url=https://www.geneseo.edu/lieutenant-governor-kathy-hochul-brings-message-college-campuses|access-date=February 16, 2018|website=www.geneseo.edu|archive-date=May 19, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170519002355/https://www.geneseo.edu/lieutenant-governor-kathy-hochul-brings-message-college-campuses|url-status=dead}}</ref> In March 2016, Cuomo named her to the New York State Women's Suffrage 100th Anniversary Commemoration Commission.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-cuomo-announces-launch-new-york-state-womens-suffrage-commission|title=Governor Cuomo Announces Launch of New York State Women's Suffrage Commission|date=August 22, 2016|access-date=February 16, 2018|archive-date=February 16, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180216084651/https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-cuomo-announces-launch-new-york-state-womens-suffrage-commission|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In 2018, Hochul supported legislation to provide driver's licenses to undocumented immigrants, a policy she had opposed as Erie County Clerk in 2007. While Hochul had said in 2007 that she would seek to have any such applicants arrested if the proposal was implemented, in 2018 she said circumstances had changed.<ref>{{cite web|last=Reisman|first=Nick|date=November 30, 2018|title=New Bill Would Allow Undocumented Migrants to Receive a Driver's License|url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/politics/2018/12/01/bill-gives-license-to-undocumented-migrants|work=[[Spectrum News]]|access-date=June 5, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Vielkind|first=Jimmy|date=May 31, 2018|title=Hochul clarifies position on immigrant driver's licenses|url=https://www.politico.com/states/new-york/albany/story/2018/05/30/hochul-clarifies-position-on-immigrant-drivers-licenses-443416|work=[[Politico]]|access-date=June 5, 2021}}</ref>

On May 30, 2018, State Senate Democrats hoped to force a vote on an abortion rights bill known as the [[Reproductive Health Act]]. Hochul was prepared to cast a tie-breaking vote on a hostile amendment; with one Republican senator away from Albany on military duty, the Senate Republican Conference and the Senate Democratic conference each had 31 members in the chamber. But Senate Republicans "abruptly shut down business and pulled all the bills for the day" when Hochul entered the Senate chamber. She called the GOP's actions "reprehensible" and "cowardly", adding, "The governor and I are offended by the actions taken here today in the Senate."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wamc.org/new-york-news/2018-05-31/gridlock-and-finger-pointing-in-ny-senate|title=Gridlock And Finger Pointing In NY Senate|date=May 31, 2018|website=WAMC}}</ref>

Hochul presided over the Senate chamber on June 5, 2018, when Senate Republicans called for the override of Cuomo's veto of a bill relating to full-day kindergarten classes. The override passed by a large margin, and was the first veto override of Cuomo's gubernatorial tenure.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.timesunion.com/news/article/Senate-touts-historic-override-of-governor-s-12969095.php|title=Deadlocked Senate continues political gamesmanship|first1=Rachel|last1=Silberstein|first2=David|last2=Lombardo|date=June 5, 2018|website=Times Union}}</ref>

At a July 2018 rally with Planned Parenthood, Hochul called upon the Republican-led State Senate to reconvene in Albany to pass the Reproductive Health Act. She asserted that the potential confirmation of then-Supreme Court nominee [[Brett Kavanaugh]] made this necessary.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/buffalo/politics/2018/07/23/hochul-planned-parenthood-roe-v--wade|title=Hochul urges passage of Reproductive Health Act|website=Spectrum News}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wbfo.org/local/2018-07-23/hochul-pushes-reproductive-health-act|title=Hochul pushes Reproductive Health Act|date=July 23, 2018|website=WBFO}}</ref> The Act was reintroduced in January 2019 and passed that month with wide margins in the state House and Senate.

== Governor of New York (2021–present) ==
[[File:Kathy Hochul swearing in 2021.jpg|thumb|Hochul being sworn in as [[Governor of New York]] on August 24, 2021]]
In a press briefing on August 10, 2021, Andrew Cuomo announced his resignation as [[Governor of New York|governor]], effective August 24. Cuomo was accused of multiple instances of [[sexual misconduct]].<ref>{{cite web|date=August 10, 2021|title=Live Updates: Governor Cuomo Says He Will Resign|url=https://www.nytimes.com/live/2021/08/10/nyregion/cuomo-resigns|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210810175327/https://www.nytimes.com/live/2021/08/10/nyregion/cuomo-resigns|archive-date=August 10, 2021|access-date=August 10, 2021|website=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Andrew Cuomo To Resign After Investigation Finds He Sexually Harassed Multiple Women|url=https://www.npr.org/2021/08/10/972725388/new-york-governor-andrew-cuomo-resigns-amid-sexual-harassment-claims|access-date=August 10, 2021|website=NPR.org|date=August 10, 2021|language=en|last1=Diaz|first1=Jaclyn}}</ref> Hochul said that [[Attorney General of New York|New York Attorney General]] [[Letitia James]]'s report on Cuomo documented "repulsive and unlawful behavior"<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://buffalonews.com/news/local/state-and-regional/hochul-berates-cuomos-repulsive-and-unlawful-behavior-while-she-plots-her-future/article_e8151d4e-f48c-11eb-8f1a-331ae1c2b2a6.html|title=Hochul berates Cuomo's 'repulsive and unlawful behavior' – while she plots her future|first=Jerry Zremski News Washington Bureau|last=Chief|website=The Buffalo News|date=August 3, 2021 }}</ref> and praised his decision to resign.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/cuomo-resignation-reaction-0798f7cb26ea486b9647e66cf1ebb215|title=As Cuomo prepares to step down, others begin looking forward|date=August 10, 2021|website=AP NEWS}}</ref> Of her time as lieutenant governor and relationship with Cuomo, Hochul has said: "I think it's very clear that the governor and I have not been close."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://buffalonews.com/news/local/government-and-politics/kathy-hochul-the-governor-and-i-have-not-been-close/article_420a4bc6-fad2-11eb-9dbc-47315bc01ce9.html|title=Kathy Hochul: 'The governor and I have not been close'|first=Maki |last=Becker |website=The Buffalo News|date=August 11, 2021 }}</ref>

Hochul was sworn in as governor at 12:00 AM [[Eastern Time Zone|Eastern Time (ET)]] on August 24 by [[List of chief judges of the New York Court of Appeals|New York Chief Judge]] [[Janet DiFiore]] in a private ceremony. A public ceremonial event was held later that morning at the [[New York State Capitol|State Capitol's]] Red Room.<ref name="SwornIn">{{Cite news |last=Ferré-Sadurní |first=Luis |date=August 24, 2021 |title=Kathy Hochul Is Sworn In as New York's First Female Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/24/nyregion/kathy-hochul-governor-ny.html |access-date=August 24, 2021 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Hochul is the state's first female governor.<ref name="SwornIn" /><ref>{{Cite web|last=Krieg|first=Gregory|date=August 23, 2021|title=Kathy Hochul becomes governor of New York as Cuomo leaves in disgrace|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/08/23/politics/kathy-hochul-new-york-governor/index.html|access-date=August 25, 2021|website=CNN}}</ref> She is also the first New York governor from outside [[New York City]] and its [[New York metropolitan area|immediate suburbs]] since 1932 (when [[Franklin D. Roosevelt|Franklin Delano Roosevelt]] left office).<ref name="SwornIn" /> Hochul also became the first governor from north of [[Hyde Park, New York|Hyde Park]] since [[Nathan L. Miller]] in 1922, in addition to being the first governor from [[Western New York]] since [[Horace White]] in 1910 and the first governor from Buffalo since [[Grover Cleveland]] in 1885.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.democratandchronicle.com/story/news/2017/12/12/last-upstate-new-york-governor-ny/943667001/|title= Upstate, schmupstate: Who was the last governor from upstate New York?|author=Steve Orr|newspaper=Democrat and Chronicle|date=August 10, 2021|access-date=December 25, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Michel|first=Clifford|date=August 10, 2021|title=Who is Kathy Hochul? Meet New York's Incoming, History-Making Governor|website=The City|url=https://www.thecity.nyc/2021/8/5/22612186/who-is-kathy-hochul-poised-to-replace-cuomo-ny-governor|access-date=August 10, 2021}}</ref> On August 12, Hochul confirmed that she planned to run for a full term as governor in [[2022 New York gubernatorial election|2022]].<ref>{{cite web|author=Bill Mahoney|date=August 12, 2021|title=Hochul confirms plans to run for governor in 2022|url=https://www.politico.com/states/new-york/albany/story/2021/08/12/hochul-confirms-plans-to-run-for-governor-in-2022-1389848|work=[[Politico]]|access-date=August 12, 2021}}</ref> She was the first Democrat to announce a 2022 gubernatorial candidacy after Cuomo said he would resign.<ref>{{cite news|last=Doherty|first=Erin|date=August 12, 2021|title=Hochul to run for New York governor in 2022 after serving out Cuomo's term|work=[[Axios (website)|Axios]]|url=https://www.axios.com/kathy-hochul-governor-2022-cuomo-new-york-c4a6d6b9-b31c-49b9-93bf-7f7f9b74441a.html|access-date=August 24, 2021|archive-date=August 24, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210824144930/https://www.axios.com/kathy-hochul-governor-2022-cuomo-new-york-c4a6d6b9-b31c-49b9-93bf-7f7f9b74441a.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== 2022 election ===
{{Further|2022 New York gubernatorial election}}
On February 17, 2022, the New York State Democratic Convention endorsed Hochul for governor. As of that month, she had raised $21 million in campaign funds.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://wskg.org/convention-officially-chooses-hochul-as-democratic-partys-designated-nominee-for-governor/|title=Convention officially chooses Hochul as Democratic Party's designated nominee for governor|last=Clark|first=Dan|date=February 18, 2022|website=WSKG|access-date=February 19, 2022|archive-date=June 29, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220629024444/https://wskg.org/convention-officially-chooses-hochul-as-democratic-partys-designated-nominee-for-governor/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Hochul won a full term in office, defeating [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee [[Lee Zeldin]] in the closest New York gubernatorial election since [[1994 New York gubernatorial election|1994]] and the closest Democratic victory since [[1982 New York gubernatorial election|1982]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Barkan |first=Ross |date=June 29, 2022 |title=Does Lee Zeldin Stand a Chance Against Kathy Hochul? |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2022/06/does-lee-zeldin-stand-a-chance-against-kathy-hochul.html |access-date=July 20, 2022 |website=Intelligencer |language=en-us}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Kathy Hochul ekes out a victory in the closest governor's race in decades |url=https://www.cityandstateny.com/politics/2022/11/kathy-hochul-ekes-out-victory-closest-governors-race-decades/379511/ |access-date=December 31, 2022 |website=City & State NY |date=November 9, 2022 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Interactive: How New Yorkers voted in the 2022 election for governor |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/politics/2022/11/09/interactive--how-new-yorkers-voted-in-the-2022-election-for-governor |access-date=December 31, 2022 |website=Spectrum News |language=en}}</ref> Hochul's election marked the first time a woman was elected governor of New York.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2022 New York governor's race: Kathy Hochul projected winner over Lee Zeldin |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/election-2022-new-york-governor-results-kathy-hochul-lee-zeldin-2022-11-08/ |access-date=December 27, 2022 |website=www.cbsnews.com |date=November 9, 2022 |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Tenure===
In August 2021, ''[[The Daily Beast]]'' and ''[[The Buffalo News]]'' reported on a potential [[conflict of interest]] between Hochul's role as governor and the high-level executive position held by her husband, [[William Hochul]], at [[Delaware North]], a Buffalo-based casino and hospitality company.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/kathy-hochul-andrew-cuomos-successor-at-new-york-governor-is-staring-at-a-massive-conflict-of-interest|title=NY's New Guv Is Staring at a Massive Conflict of Interest|first=William|last=Bredderman|newspaper=The Daily Beast|date=August 11, 2021|via=www.thedailybeast.com}}</ref> Delaware North has stated that William Hochul will be prohibited from working on any matter that involves state business, oversight, or regulation. A spokesman for Kathy Hochul has said that she had a [[recusal]] process as lieutenant governor and would maintain that process as governor.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://buffalonews.com/news/local/state-and-regional/kathy-hochuls-husband-will-stay-at-delaware-north-despite-potential-conflict-of-interest/article_07093c5e-fab3-11eb-86ce-a719b598c960.html|title=Kathy Hochul's husband will stay at Delaware North despite potential conflict of interest|last=Watson|first=Stephen P.|date=August 11, 2021|work=Buffalo News|access-date=August 25, 2021}}</ref>

On August 26, 2021, Hochul appointed State Senator [[Brian Benjamin]] to the position of [[lieutenant governor of New York]].<ref name = syracuse>{{Cite web|url=https://www.syracuse.com/state/2021/08/gov-kathy-hochul-officially-names-brian-benjamin-as-ny-lieutenant-governor.html|title=Gov. Kathy Hochul officially names her NY lieutenant governor|date=August 26, 2021|website=syracuse.com}}</ref> Benjamin was sworn in on September 9, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://nypost.com/2021/09/09/brian-benjamin-sworn-in-as-new-yorks-lieutenant-governor/|title=Brian Benjamin sworn in to replace Hochul as NY's lieutenant governor|first=Bernadette|last=Hogan|date=September 9, 2021}}</ref><ref name= DN>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nydailynews.com/news/politics/new-york-elections-government/ny-hochul-appoints-brian-benjamin-as-ny-lieutenant-governor-20210909-5wjmahfd4jgmjiqeujau55j3lq-story.html|title=Brian Benjamin sworn in as N.Y. lieutenant governor but questions loom about wedding, stock|first=Michael|last=Gartland|website=nydailynews.com |date=September 9, 2021| access-date=September 9, 2021}}</ref> Hochul also appointed [[Karen Persichilli Keogh]] to be secretary to the governor and [[Kathryn Garcia]] to be director of state operations.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cityandstateny.com/personality/2021/11/how-karen-persichilli-keogh-became-secretary-governor/186680/|title=How Karen Persichilli Keogh became secretary to the governor|date=November 8, 2021|website=City & State NY}}</ref> In October 2021 Houchul endorsed [[Eric Adams]] in the [[2021 New York City mayoral election]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-10-29 |title=Gov. Hochul, Sen. Schumer shower love on Eric Adams ahead of NYC mayoral election |url=https://www.nydailynews.com/2021/10/29/gov-hochul-sen-schumer-shower-love-on-eric-adams-ahead-of-nyc-mayoral-election/ |access-date=2025-09-02 |website=New York Daily News |language=en-US}}</ref>

[[File:Governor Hochul and MTA Leadership Take LIRR Ride Direct to Grand Central (51644165041).jpg|thumb|Hochul discussing [[East Side Access]] in October 2021]]
In November 2021, Hochul pushed to end remote work during the [[COVID-19 pandemic in New York (state)|COVID-19 pandemic]] and to return workers to offices.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://abc7ny.com/kathy-hochul-manhattan-officer-workers-survey-partnership-of-new-york/11221574/|title=Gov. Hochul urges NYC employers to bring workers back to the office|access-date=December 3, 2021|date=November 11, 2021|work=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]}}</ref> That same month, Hochul offered her plans to redevelop Manhattan's [[Pennsylvania Station (New York City)|Pennsylvania Station]] and the surrounding neighborhood. In her plans, she called for reducing density in the area.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Hochul seeks 'wow factor' in her vision for Penn Station|url=https://www.newsday.com/long-island/transportation/penn-station-plan-hochul-1.50410187|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211103185345/https://www.newsday.com/long-island/transportation/penn-station-plan-hochul-1.50410187|url-status=dead|archive-date=November 3, 2021|access-date=November 4, 2021|website=Newsday|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=2021|title=Governor Unveils Plan to Fix—and Rename—NYC's Penn Station|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-11-03/hochul-unveils-plan-to-revamp-nyc-s-depressing-penn-station|access-date=November 4, 2021|website=www.bloomberg.com}}</ref>

In December 2021, Hochul announced the reinstatement of an indoor [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic|mask mandate]] amid the spread of the [[SARS-CoV-2 Omicron variant|Omicron variant]].<ref>{{Cite web|title= New York Gov. Kathy Hochul imposes statewide mask mandate amid spread of omicron Covid variant|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2021/12/10/ny-gov-kathy-hochul-imposes-statewide-mask-mandate-amid-spread-of-omicron-covid-variant.html|access-date=December 11, 2021|website=CNBC|date=December 10, 2021|language=en}}</ref>

In January 2022, she expanded an existing vaccine mandate for healthcare workers to include a [[Booster dose|booster shot]] requirement.<ref>{{Cite web|title= NY to Require Booster Shots for Health Care Workers; No Test-Out Option|url=https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/coronavirus/ny-to-require-booster-shots-for-health-care-workers-no-test-out-option/3485247/|access-date=January 8, 2022|website=NBC|date=January 7, 2022 |language=en}}</ref> Also in January 2022, Hochul confirmed that New York's eviction [[Moratorium (law)|moratorium]] would expire on January 15. She announced that she would sign on to a letter with other governors to the federal government asking for more rent assistance, after New York received only $27.2 million of its nearly $1 billion request. Tenant advocates and other politicians have pushed her and state lawmakers to pass the [[good cause eviction]] bill, which would give tenants the right to a lease renewal in most cases, [[rent regulation|cap rent increases]], and require landlords to obtain a judge's order to evict tenants.<ref>{{Cite web|title= Hochul confirms eviction moratorium concluding this weekend|url=https://www.amny.com/news/hochul-confirms-eviction-moratorium-concluding-this-weekend/|access-date=January 11, 2022|website=AMNY|date=January 11, 2022|language=en}}</ref>

In March 2022, Hochul reached an agreement with the [[Buffalo Bills]] to have taxpayers pay $850 million for the construction of a [[Future Buffalo Bills stadium|new stadium]], as well as commit to maintain and repair the stadium.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Ferré-Sadurní |first=Luis |date=March 28, 2022 |title=Buffalo Bills Strike Deal for Taxpayer-Funded $1.4 Billion Stadium |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/28/nyregion/buffalo-bills-stadium-deal.html |access-date=March 28, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |author=Stephen T. Watson |title=Here is the cost breakdown for new Bills stadium |url=https://buffalonews.com/news/local/here-is-the-cost-breakdown-for-new-bills-stadium/article_532872e2-ae9c-11ec-bd57-5f0465cd6118.html |access-date=March 28, 2022 |website=Buffalo News |date=March 28, 2022 |language=en}}</ref> It was set to be the largest taxpayer contribution ever for a [[National Football League]] facility.<ref name=":0" /> The agreement was released four days before the state budget was due to be passed, making it hard for lawmakers to scrutinize it.<ref name=":0" /> Critics of the agreement characterized it as [[corporate welfare]].<ref name=":0" /> Part of the funding for the stadium came from a payment from the [[Seneca Nation of New York]], whose bank accounts had been recently frozen as part of a long-running dispute between the Seneca Nation and the State of New York over the Seneca Nation's refusal to pay certain fees related to casino gaming despite being ordered to do so by multiple judicial bodies.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 30, 2022 |title=Seneca Nation speaks out on Hochul's plan to use casino compact money for new Bills stadium |url=https://www.wivb.com/sports/buffalo-bills-stadium-discussions/seneca-nation-leaders-and-community-speaking-out-about-the-governors-plan-to-use-casino-compact-money-for-the-new-stadium/ |access-date=March 31, 2022 |website=News 4 Buffalo |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=January 13, 2022 |title=Seneca Nation announces settlement of longtime casino revenue dispute with NYS |url=https://www.wbfo.org/state/2022-01-13/seneca-nation-announces-settlement-of-longtime-casino-revenue-dispute-with-nys |access-date=July 2, 2022 |website=WBFO |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=January 13, 2022 |title=Seneca Nation settles multi-million dollar casino dispute with state |url=https://www.newyorkupstate.com/casinos/2022/01/seneca-nation-settles-multi-million-dollar-casino-dispute-seeks-new-gaming-pact-with-ny.html |access-date=July 2, 2022 |website=newyorkupstate |language=en}}</ref>
[[File:P20220203AS-1684 (51918008026).jpg|thumb|right|Hochul with President [[Joe Biden]] and New York City Mayor [[Eric Adams]] in February 2022]]
On April 12, 2022, Brian Benjamin resigned as lieutenant governor after having been indicted earlier that day on federal charges of [[bribery]], [[conspiracy]] to commit wire fraud, [[Mail and wire fraud|wire fraud]], and falsification of records.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 12, 2022 |title=NY lieutenant governor resigns after arrest in federal probe |url=https://apnews.com/article/brian-benjamin-new-york-lieutenant-governor-arrested-3682bc9913a2421f1b1613497286b591 |access-date=April 12, 2022 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Rashbaum |first1=William K. |last2=Fandos |first2=Nicholas |last3=Mays |first3=Jeffery C. |date=April 12, 2022 |title=Lt. Gov. Benjamin Resigns Following Campaign Finance Indictment |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/04/12/nyregion/brian-benjamin-resigns-indicted.html |access-date=April 13, 2022 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |first=Sonia |last=Moghe |title=NY lieutenant governor indicted on multiple charges |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/04/12/politics/brian-benjamin-new-york/index.html |access-date=April 12, 2022 |website=CNN|date=April 12, 2022 }}</ref> The crimes of which Benjamin is accused were allegedly committed during his State Senate tenure.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/ny-lieutenant-governor-brian-benjamin-arrested-bribery-charges|title=NY lieutenant governor arrested on bribery charges|date=April 12, 2022|access-date=April 12, 2022|website=foxnews.com|last1=Dhanis|first1=Marta|last2=Blitzer|first2=Ronn}}</ref> He has pleaded not guilty to all charges.<ref>{{Cite news |date=April 12, 2022 |title=NY lieutenant governor Benjamin pleads not guilty to bribery, related charges |language=en |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/ny-lieutenant-governor-benjamin-pleads-not-guilty-bribery-related-charges-2022-04-12/ |access-date=April 12, 2022}}</ref>

On May 3, 2022, Hochul selected U.S. Representative [[Antonio Delgado]] to serve as [[Lieutenant Governor of New York|lieutenant governor]] of New York.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.news10.com/news/ny-capitol-news/congressman-antonio-delgado-chosen-to-be-next-lieutenant-governor |title=Congressman Antonio Delgado Chosen to Be Next Lieutenant Governor |publisher=[[WTEN]] |date=May 4, 2022 |access-date=May 4, 2022 |first=Jamie |last=DeLine |quote=Although an exact date has not been set, Governor Hochul said he will be sworn in sometime this month.}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Governor Hochul Announces Appointment of Representative Antonio Delgado as Lieutenant Governor |url=https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-hochul-announces-appointment-representative-antonio-delgado-lieutenant-governor |access-date=May 3, 2022 |date=May 3, 2022 |first=Kathy |last=Hochul |publisher=[[Governor of New York]] |language=en}}</ref> Delgado was sworn in on May 25.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Smith |first=Lindsey |date=May 25, 2022 |title=Rep. Antonio Delgado sworn in as NY's lieutenant governor |url=https://cbs6albany.com/news/local/rep-antonio-delgado-to-be-sworn-in-as-nys-lieutenant-governor}}</ref>

On June 22, 2022, Hochul announced a $300 million plan to rebuild infrastructure in western New York communities with public and private funding.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 2, 2022 |title=WNY to receive $300 million to help disadvantaged neighborhoods |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/buffalo/news/2022/06/02/western-new-york-to-receive--300-million-to-help-disadvantaged-neighborhoods |access-date=June 27, 2022 |website=Spectrum News |language=en}}</ref>

In 2022, a citizens' group called Uniting NYS and a group of state legislators sued Hochul in the [[New York State Supreme Court]] in ''George M. Borrello et al. v. Kathleen C. Hochul et al.'' to prevent implementation of a recently created state department of health policy enabling health practitioners to refer even asymptomatic patients suspected of having been exposed to a contagious illness for possible involuntary detention backed by law enforcement, with the option of holding detainees incommunicado.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Borrello et al v. Hochul et al |url=https://dockets.justia.com/docket/new-york/nywdce/1:2022cv00281/140682 |access-date=September 3, 2022 |website=Justia Dockets & Filings |language=en}}</ref> The policy had been created without consulting the state legislature and did not require an emergency to be implemented. New York Supreme Court Justice Ronald Ploetz ruled against Hochul on the grounds of unconstitutionality under the [[separation of powers]] doctrine of both the state and federal constitutions, as well as on the grounds that the policy was cruel and lacked [[due process]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.post-journal.com/news/page-one/2022/07/judge-rules-proposed-isolation-rules-are-unconstitutional/ | title=Judge Rules Proposed Isolation Rules Are Unconstitutional }}</ref> The New York state attorney general and Hochul are filing an appeal to retain the regulations, and the appeal is being challenged.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.observertoday.com/news/local-region/2022/07/appeal-filed-in-borrello-led-case/ | title=Appeal filed in Borrello-led case }}</ref>

In late 2022, Hochul delayed the signing of the Digital Fair Repair Act, a bill that received rare bipartisan support. The delay was allegedly due to major equipment manufacturers' lobbying efforts.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Purdy |first=Kevin |date=December 19, 2022 |title=Lobbyists have held up nation's first right-to-repair bill in New York |url=https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2022/12/right-to-repair-bill-passed-in-june-still-awaits-ny-governors-signature/ |access-date=December 20, 2022 |website=Ars Technica |language=en-us}}</ref> On December 28, 2022, Hochul signed the Digital Fair Repair Act (Senate bill 4104-A) into law, but not before adding an amendment that equipment manufacturers "may provide assemblies of parts rather than individual components".<ref>Signed bill S4104-A by Governor Hochul with amendment [https://pbs.twimg.com/media/FlHtbaRWAAEdwdv?format=jpg&name=large Image of Signed Bill]</ref> Independent repair analysts such as [[Louis Rossmann]] have claimed these amendments undermine the bill's purpose, and allege codification into law of "unethical practices".<ref>{{Citation |title=Governor Hochul SABOTAGES NY right to repair bill, right on schedule | date=December 28, 2022 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7xGBB-717AI |language=en |access-date=December 29, 2022}}</ref>

==== First full term ====
Hochul was inaugurated on January 1, 2023.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Ferré-Sadurní |first1=Luis |last2=McKinley |first2=Jesse |date=January 1, 2023 |title=At Inauguration, Hochul Vows to Make New York Safer and More Affordable |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/01/01/nyregion/hochul-inauguration-ny-governor.html |access-date=January 18, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In 2023, Hochul nominated [[Hector LaSalle]] for the vacant position of chief judge of the [[New York Court of Appeals]]. LaSalle's nomination drew opposition from a wide variety of Democratic groups and constituencies, including numerous unions and trade groups, criminal justice advocates, elected officeholders, and local party affiliates, who raised concerns about his track record on issues relating to abortion, criminal justice, corporate interests, and the environment. Many also suggested that he would do little to stem, or could even accelerate, the Court of Appeals's rightward drift under the previous chief judge, [[Janet DiFiore]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ahead of crucial hearing, Hochul pitches LaSalle |url=https://www.cityandstateny.com/politics/2023/01/ahead-crucial-hearing-hochul-pitches-lasalle/381862/ |access-date=January 18, 2023 |website=City & State NY |date=January 17, 2023 |language=en}}</ref> Despite an extensive lobbying campaign by Hochul's allies, including support from [[Party leaders of the United States House of Representatives|U.S. House Minority Leader]] [[Hakeem Jeffries]], the Senate Judiciary Committee rejected LaSalle's nomination by a 10–9 vote.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Ferré-Sadurní |first1=Luis |last2=McKinley |first2=Jesse |date=January 18, 2023 |title=LaSalle Is Rejected by New York Senate Panel in a 10-9 Vote |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/01/18/nyregion/chief-judge-lasalle-hearing.html |access-date=January 18, 2023 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Hochul subsequently sued to demand a full floor vote. Democratic leadership then held a full Senate floor vote, which overwhelmingly rejected LaSalle's nomination.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/15/nyregion/hector-lasalle-chief-judge-vote.html | title=State Senate Rejects Nominee for Chief Judge in Defeat for Hochul | work=The New York Times | date=February 15, 2023 | last1=Ferré-Sadurní | first1=Luis }}</ref> Hochul proceeded to nominate associate judge [[Rowan D. Wilson]] as chief judge and [[Caitlin Halligan]] as the replacement associate judge for Wilson. The [[New York Senate]] confirmed the nominations on April 19, 2023.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/ny-state-of-politics/2023/04/19/caitlin-halligan-confirmed-for-new-york-s-top-court | title=Caitlin Halligan confirmed for New York's top court }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.wxxinews.org/capitol-bureau/2023-04-18/rowan-wilson-confirmed-as-new-yorks-first-african-american-chief-judge | title=Rowan Wilson confirmed as New York's first African American chief judge | date=April 18, 2023 }}</ref>

Hochul has employed consulting firms [[Deloitte]] and [[Boston Consulting Group]] in preparing her State of the State address, an arrangement that ''The New York Times'' in April 2023 called "unusual, and possibly novel".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/04/14/nyregion/hochul-consultants-state-of-state.html|title=Hochul Paid $2 Million for Outside Help on State of the State Speeches|first1=Nicholas|last1=Fandos|first2=Jay|last2=Root|work=The New York Times |date=April 15, 2023|via=NYTimes.com}}</ref> In December 2023, Hochul vetoed legislation to ban [[Non-compete clause|non-compete agreements]]. Business interests lobbied her to veto it while labor supported it, arguing that non-compete agreements harm workers' interests.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Solomon |first=Joshua |date=2023-12-23 |title=Hochul vetoes noncompete ban that was opposed by business sector |url=https://www.timesunion.com/state/article/hochul-sides-business-lobby-vetoes-noncompete-ban-18571186.php |website=Times Union |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Ferré-Sadurní |first=Luis |date=2023-12-22 |title=Hochul Vetoes Ban on Noncompete Agreements in New York |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/22/nyregion/kathy-hochul-veto-noncompete.html |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In February 2024, during the [[Israeli invasion of the Gaza Strip (2023–present)|Israeli invasion of Gaza]], Hochul was the keynote speaker at a [[United Jewish Appeal]]-Federation of New York event. During her speech, she made remarks implying that [[Israel]] had a right to destroy [[Gaza Strip|Gaza]] in response to the October 7, 2023, [[2023 Hamas-led attack on Israel|Hamas attack on Israel]]: "If Canada someday ever attacked Buffalo, I'm sorry, my friends, there would be no Canada the next day."<ref name="gaza_2024_02_16_nytimes">[https://www.nytimes.com/2024/02/16/nyregion/kathy-hochul-israel-gaza-canada-comments.html "Hochul Apologizes After Implying Israel Has a Right to Destroy Gaza:  In remarks made at a Jewish philanthropy event, Gov. Kathy Hochul said that if Canada attacked the United States as Hamas did Israel, 'there would be no Canada the next day',"] February 16, 2024, ''[[The New York Times]],'' February 16, 2024</ref><ref name="gaza_2024_02_18_cbc_ca">{{cite web |title=New York governor apologizes for implying it is 'Israel's right' to destroy Gaza |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/new-york-governor-apologizes-israel-gaza-canada-buffalo-kathy-hochul-1.7118711 |website=CBC |publisher=Reuters |access-date=18 February 2024}}</ref><ref name="gaza_cnn">[https://www.cnn.com/2024/02/17/politics/kathy-hochul-canada-hamas-israel/index.html " New York Gov. Kathy Hochul apologizes for using Canada-Hamas analogy in defending Israel,"] February 17, 2024, [[Cable News Network|CNN]], retrieved February 19m 2024</ref> Following angry responses from a wide range of parties—including public officials and the Buffalo chapter of [[Jewish Voice for Peace]]—Hochul apologized for her "inappropriate analogy".<ref name="gaza_2024_02_16_nytimes" /><ref name="gaza_2024_02_18_cbc_ca"/>

In March 2024, Hochul instituted a policy in coordination with [[Eric Adams]] to deploy 750 National Guard soldiers to the NYC subway system and empower them to conduct random bag checks on subway-goers. She also put forth legislation to allow judges to ban criminals from riding the subway.<ref>{{Cite news |work=NBC |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/gov-kathy-hochul-sending-national-guard-members-new-york-city-subways-rcna142063 |title=Gov. Kathy Hochul sending National Guard members to New York City subways to combat ongoing crime}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |work=CBS |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/newyork/news/national-guard-nyc-subway-bag-checks/ |title=Hochul deploys National Guard troops to New York City subway amid rising crime numbers}}</ref> In May 2024, Hochul was criticized for saying at a business conference that minority children growing up in the [[Bronx]] "don't even know what the word 'computer' is."<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-07 |title=New York governor regrets saying Black kids in the Bronx don't know what a computer is |url=https://apnews.com/article/hochul-black-kids-bronx-computer-comment-e0c3c69b1ebf9639d8f9e402096f8446 |access-date=2024-08-30 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> She later apologized, saying she misspoke and regretted her remark.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ashford |first=Grace |date=2024-05-07 |title=Hochul Regrets Saying Some 'Black Kids' Don't Know the Word 'Computer' |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/05/06/nyregion/hochul-black-kids-computer.html |access-date=2024-08-30 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

In June 2024, Hochul indefinitely halted implementation of a [[Congestion pricing in New York City|congestion pricing]] plan in the most congested parts of Manhattan just weeks before it was to go into effect.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ashford |first=Grace |date=2024-06-05 |title=Hochul Halts Congestion Pricing in a Stunning 11th-Hour Shift |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/06/05/nyregion/congestion-pricing-pause-hochul.html |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The plan was approved in 2019 and was expected to bring in $1 billion in revenue per year, making it the MTA's largest source of funding.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Stack |first=Liam |date=2024-06-05 |title=What Happens Now That Congestion Pricing Has Been Halted |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/06/05/nyregion/what-happens-next-congestion-pricing.html |access-date=2024-06-05 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> At the time of the cancellation, the MTA had already purchased $500 million for equipment needed for congestion pricing and earmarked $15 billion for projects it expected to fund with the revenue.<ref name=":1" /> Hochul proposed raising the city's [[payroll tax]] for business owners to cover the lost revenue from the cancellation of the congestion pricing,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://news.bloombergtax.com/daily-tax-report/ny-lawmakers-weigh-business-tax-hike-after-hochul-toll-reversal |title=NY Lawmakers Weigh Business Tax Hike After Hochul Toll Reversal |author1=Kaske, Michelle |author2=Muoio Dunn, Danielle |work=Bloomberg |date=June 6, 2024 |access-date=June 6, 2024}}</ref> which New York lawmakers declined to consider.<ref>{{cite web |last=Garber |first=Nick |date=June 6, 2024 |title=Hochul's business tax proposal falters in Albany after congestion pricing pause |url=https://www.crainsnewyork.com/politics-policy/hochuls-business-tax-proposal-falters-albany-after-congestion-pricing-pause |access-date=June 7, 2024 |website=Crain's New York Business |postscript=none}}; {{Cite web |last=Hogan |first=Bernadette |date=June 6, 2024 |title=State lawmakers reject Hochul's NYC business tax proposal |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/politics/2024/06/06/state-lawmakers-reject-hochul-s-nyc-business-tax-proposal-in-wake-of-congestion-pricing-delay |access-date=June 7, 2024 |website=Spectrum News}}</ref> Her decision received widespread backlash, especially from state lawmakers,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.northcountrypublicradio.org/news/story/49959/20240606/backlash-to-hochul-s-decision-to-halt-congestion-pricing-delays-the-end-of-ny-legislative-session |title=Backlash to Hochul's decision to halt congestion pricing delays the end of NY legislative session |author=Dewitt, Karen |work=[[North Country Public Radio]] |date=June 6, 2024 |access-date=June 6, 2024}}</ref> business leaders,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thecity.nyc/2024/06/05/congestion-fee-manhattan-hochul-business/ |title=Business Leaders 'Furious' at Hochul Reversal on Manhattan Congestion Charge |author=David, Greg |work=[[THe City (website)|The City]] |date=June 5, 2024 |access-date=June 6, 2024}}</ref> and climate activists.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://grist.org/transportation/kathy-hochul-congestion-pricing-new-york/ |title=Caving on climate: Kathy Hochul axes congestion pricing in New York |author=Bittle, Jake |work=[[Grist (magazine)|Grist]] |date=June 5, 2024 |access-date=June 6, 2024}}</ref> Hochul defended her sudden decision to block the congestion pricing plan based in part on conversations with New Yorkers, particularly from a Manhattan diner, and said the plan would "suck the vitality out of this city when we're still fighting for our comeback". Lawmakers said her plan to raise payroll taxes to cover the lost revenue ran contrary to her reason for halting the program and that the sudden tax hike would incapacitate the city's post-COVID-19 economic recovery. Democratic Senate Deputy Leader [[Michael Gianaris]] condemned how the program was derailed "at the last possible moment" before giving lawmakers less than 48 hours to create an alternative funding plan, calling it "irresponsible" and "inconsistent with principles of good governance".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-06-08 |title=New York governor defends blocking plan that would toll Manhattan drivers to pay for subway repairs |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-york-congestion-pricing-hochul-subway-56c1661a9c1f83c69af644e02f49b9d1 |access-date=2024-06-08 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> Senator [[Zellnor Myrie]] called it "incomprehensible" that Hochul claimed to care about cost of living while proposing a tax hike.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-06-06 |title=New York governor pushes for tax increase after nixing toll program in Manhattan |url=https://apnews.com/article/new-york-congestion-pricing-hochul-446183f1b5fb829f04f2316cc56c8944 |access-date=2024-06-08 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> In November 2024, Hochul revived the congestion pricing plan with the charge for most passenger vehicles reduced from $15 to $9;<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Volack |first1=Jason |last2=Katersky |first2=Aaron |title=New York City congestion pricing plan returns with new reduced tolls |url=https://abcnews.go.com/US/new-york-city-congestion-pricing-plan-returns-new/story?id=115868701 |access-date=2024-12-05 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref> it was implemented on January 5, 2025.

In June 2024, Hochul proposed banning face masks on the [[New York City Subway]] in response to groups of protesters she said were conducting antisemitic acts and confronting people while covering their faces. Organizations such as the [[New York Civil Liberties Union]] criticized the proposal as a means to target protesters who hide their identities to avoid legal or professional repercussions, saying it would be selectively used to "arrest, doxx, surveil, and silence people of color and protesters the police disagree with".<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-06-13 |title=Gov. Hochul considering a face mask ban on New York City subways, citing antisemitic acts |url=https://apnews.com/article/mask-ban-new-york-city-subway-7c83637a00c816407d1174145fc64339 |access-date=2024-06-14 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>

In July 2024, Hochul said she intends to run for reelection as governor in 2026.<ref name=HochulRunning>{{Cite web |last=Parsnow |first=Luke |date=2024-07-02 |title=Hochul says she's running for another term as New York governor in 2026 |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/politics/2024/07/02/hochul-says-she-s-running-for-another-term-as-governor-in-2026 |access-date=2024-08-20 |website=Spectrum News 1 |language=en |archive-date=July 8, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240708215410/https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/politics/2024/07/02/hochul-says-she-s-running-for-another-term-as-governor-in-2026 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In September 2024, Hochul signed The Retail Worker Safety Act.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.riverdalepress.com/stories/us-rep-ritchie-torres-reintroduces-bodega-act,173053|title=U.S. Rep. Ritchie Torres reintroduces BODEGA Act|first=Steven|last=Goodstein|date=March 7, 2025|website=The Riverdale Press}}</ref> In December 2024, Hochul vetoed a bill sponsored by Senator [[Joseph Addabbo Jr.]] that would speed up the licensing of three new casinos in Queens<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.qchron.com/editions/queenswide/hochul-vetoes-casino-license-legislation/article_0331c3a8-fec8-5068-960f-aa58ed2ec7f8.html|title=Hochul vetoes casino license legislation|last=Guglielmo|first=Kristen |date=December 5, 2024|website=Queens Chronicle}}</ref> and signed a law capping out-of-pocket costs for [[EpiPen]]s at $100 yearly.<ref>{{cite web | title=EpiPen out-of-pocket costs capped at $100 yearly in NYS thanks to new law | website=WENY News | date=December 12, 2024 | url=https://www.weny.com/story/51981387/epipen-outofpocket-costs-capped-at-dollar100-yearly-in-nys-thanks-to-new-law | access-date=December 13, 2024|last=Freidhoff|first=Connor}}</ref>

In January 2025, Hochul announced a proposal to provide [[Free education|free tuition]] for specific [[associate degree]] programs at [[State University of New York|State]] and [[City University of New York]] schools.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Izaguirre |first=Anthony |date=2025-01-14 |title=Hochul to announce free tuition plan at city and state colleges |url=https://www.nbcnewyork.com/new-york/free-college-hochul-suny-cuny-tuition/6106169/ |access-date=2025-01-15 |website=NBC New York |language=en-US |agency=Associated Press}}</ref> In February, she refused an extradition request from Louisiana for a New York doctor who had been indicted in Louisiana after mailing abortion pills to a woman in Louisiana, who allegedly forced her pregnant minor daughter to take them.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 February 2025 |title=New York governor rejects Louisiana extradition request for doctor accused of mailing abortion pills |url=https://lailluminator.com/2025/02/13/new-york-extradition/ |website=Louisiana Illuminator |language=en-US}}</ref> Also in 2025, Hochul directed CUNY to take down a job listing for a Palestinian studies teaching position at [[Hunter College]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Leland |first1=John |author1-link=John Leland (journalist) |title=CUNY Removes Palestinian Studies Job Listing on Hochul's Orders |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/02/27/nyregion/cuny-palestine-studies.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=February 27, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250228023835/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/02/27/nyregion/cuny-palestine-studies.html |archive-date=28 February 2025}}</ref> In December 2025, [[Ontario]] premier [[Doug Ford]] and Hochul signed a [[memorandum of understanding]] that made the [[New York Power Authority]] and [[Ontario Power Generation]] work together to advance nuclear energy technology.<ref>{{cite news |last=Lang |first=Ethan |date=19 December 2025 |title=Ontario, New York announce nuclear energy agreement |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/toronto/ford-hochul-buffalo-newser-9.7022770 |work=[[CBC News]] |access-date=4 April 2026}}</ref>

Hochul did not endorse anyone in the [[2025 New York City Democratic mayoral primary]]. On September 14, 2025, she endorsed Democratic nominee [[Zohran Mamdani]] in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-09-14 |title=Kathy Hochul: Why I Am Endorsing Zohran Mamdani |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/09/14/opinion/hochul-endorsement-mamdani.html |access-date=2025-09-14 |website=New York Times |language=en}}</ref> On January 8, 2026, Mamdani and Hochul announced a plan to increase spending by $1.7 billion to provide universal [[pre-kindergarten]] statewide and universal 3K care in [[New York City]], create a free childcare program for two-year-olds in New York City, and expand childcare subsidies.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gould |first=Jessica |last2=Vielkind |first2=Jimmy |last3=Giambrone |first3=Andrew |date=2026-01-08 |title=Gov. Hochul, Mayor Mamdani announce push for free child care for NYC 2-year-olds |url=https://gothamist.com/news/gov-hochul-mayor-mamdani-announce-push-for-free-child-care-for-nyc-2-year-olds |access-date=2026-01-13 |website=Gothamist |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Featherstone |first=Liza |date=2026-01-10 |title=Zohran Mamdani Is Already Winning on Childcare |url=https://jacobin.com/2026/01/mamdani-hochul-universal-childcare-policy |access-date=2026-01-13 |website=Jacobin |language=en-US}}</ref>

''Time'' magazine listed Hochul as one of its top 100 climate leaders in its "Time100 Climate 2025". Hochul wrote in ''Time'' that New York will not wait for the federal government to ensure a cleaner and greener environment, saying: "Since I took office in 2021, we have seen once-in-a-century storms become routine. It is up to all of us to work together to reverse this for the good of all of us, our children, and their children. Time is running short. But we must address this critical situation in a way that ensures affordability for those paying the rates and grid reliability so the lights and heat stay on."<ref>{{cite news |title=Time100 Climate 2025 |url=https://time.com/collections/time-100-climate-2025/7326528/kathy-hochul/ |publisher=Time |date=December 2025}}</ref>

In January 2026, Hochul signed an executive order allowing hospitals to break the [[2026 New York City nurses strike]] by [[Strikebreaker|hiring staff in their place]]. Travel nurses said that during this period they worked $9,000 week-long contracts, and hospitals spent $100,000 hiring strikebreakers. Striking nurses marched on Hochul's office in protest, but she extended her order several times, citing patient safety issues. Many nurses criticized her for alleviating pressure on hospitals during the strike. Nurses at the [[Mount Sinai Morningside|Mount Sinai]] and [[Montefiore Einstein Medical Center|Montefiore]] systems reached a three-year contract agreement on February 9.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Goldstein |first=Joseph |date=2026-02-09 |title=N.Y.C. Nurses Reach Deal to End Strike at Two Hospital Systems |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/09/nyregion/nyc-nurses-strike-ends.html |access-date=2026-04-06 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Nurses at [[NewYork-Presbyterian Hospital]] reached a three-year contract agreement on February 20.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Peltz |first=Jennifer |date=2026-02-22 |title=NYC nursing walkout ends as last striking nurses approve new contract |url=https://apnews.com/article/nursing-strike-newyorkpresbyterian-vote-2445cf5ac3c82be234eb3fe0fca99817 |access-date=2026-04-06 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>

{{excerpt|ICE and New York City during the second Trump presidency#May 29, 2026|only=paragraphs}}

In June 2026, Hochul launched an exploratory committee to study a potential joint [[New York City]]-[[Lake Placid, New York|Lake Placid]] bid for the 2042 [[Winter Olympics]].<ref>[https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7381433/2026/06/22/new-york-lake-placid-2042-winter-olympics-paralympics/ New York City and Lake Placid explore joint bid to host 2042 Winter Olympics and Paralympics]</ref>

==Political positions==
===Energy===
During her 2011 congressional campaign, Hochul favored offering incentives to develop [[alternative energy]].<ref name="differ on more"/>

In June 2011, Hochul opposed legislation that would cut funding for the [[Commodity Futures Trading Commission|Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC)]] by 44%, on the grounds that the CFTC curbs speculation in oil and the resulting layoffs of CFTC personnel would "make it easier for Big Oil companies and speculators to take advantage of ... consumers".<ref name="first legislation"/>

===Fiscal policy===
While running for Congress in 2011, Hochul supported raising taxes on those making more than $500,000 per year.<ref name="differ on more"/> She acknowledged during her campaign that substantial cuts must be made in the federal budget, and said she would consider cuts in all entitlement programs. But she expressed opposition to the Republican plan that would turn [[Medicare (United States)|Medicare]] into a voucher system, saying it "would end Medicare as we know it".<ref name="differ on more"/><ref name="vote smart1"/> She said money could be saved in the Medicare program by eliminating waste and purchasing prescription drugs in bulk,<ref name="differ on more"/> and that the creation of more jobs would alleviate Medicare and [[Social Security Administration|Social Security]] budget shortfalls due to increased collections of payroll taxes.<ref name="differ on more"/>

As a member of Congress, Hochul voted for a [[balanced budget amendment]], which she called "a bipartisan solution".<ref name="Balanced Budget">{{cite web|title=Hochul Splits with Party on Balanced Budget Amendment|url=http://www.wgrz.com/news/article/142358/37/Hochul-Splits-with-Party-on-Balanced-Budget-Amendment|publisher=WGRZ|access-date=March 26, 2012|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130209153037/http://www.wgrz.com/news/article/142358/37/Hochul-Splits-with-Party-on-Balanced-Budget-Amendment<!-- not archived by Wayback Machine -->|archive-date=February 9, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In July 2023, in response to a statewide workforce shortage, Governor Hochul waived exam application fees for all New York State civil service exams through the end of 2025 to make it easier to join the state workforce.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reisman |first=Nick |date=2023-07-03 |title=How New York wants to make it easier to join state workforce |url=https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/ny-state-of-politics/2023/07/03/how-new-york-wants-to-make-it-easier-to-join-state-workforce |access-date=2023-07-03 |website=Spectrum News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Shahi |first=Priya |date=2023-07-02 |title=New York state Civil Service exam fees waived through 2025 |url=https://www.nny360.com/top_stories/new-york-state-civil-service-exam-fees-waived-through-2025/article_ab86349c-342b-59f6-87e4-8ffc24db6c95.html |access-date=2023-07-03 |website=NNY360 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-07-02 |title=New York waives civil service exam fees through 2025 |url=https://www.rochesterfirst.com/new-york-state/new-york-waives-civil-service-exam-fees-through-2025/ |access-date=2023-07-03 |website=RochesterFirst |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Health care===
Hochul expressed support for the [[Affordable Care Act]] passed by the [[111th United States Congress|111th Congress]] and said during her 2011 campaign that she would not vote to repeal it.<ref name="differ on more"/> In response to a constituent's question during a [[town hall meeting]] in February 2012, she was booed for saying that the federal government was "not looking to the Constitution" under the Obama administration requirement that employers provide their workers with insurance coverage for birth control.<ref name="under fire"/><ref name="wont go away"/> A spokesman later said she had misspoken, but did not clarify her answer.<ref name="wont go away"/>

Hochul has said she is pro-choice<ref name="social issues"/> and called the overturning of ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'' a "grave injustice".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Hochul Calls Court Decision a 'Grave Injustice,' Says New Yorkers' Right to Abortion is Protected |url=https://nynow.wmht.org/blogs/politics/governor-hochul-reacts-to-supreme-court-overturning-roe-v-wade/ |access-date=2024-05-05 |website=New York NOW |language=en}}</ref>

===Immigration===
In 2023, Hochul advocated that the federal government expedite work permits to migrants to address a surge of immigration to New York, writing to President [[Joe Biden]], "For me, the answer to these two crises—a humanitarian crisis and our workforce crisis—is so crystal clear and common sense. Let them get the work authorizations; let them work; legally, let them work."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Cordero |first1=Katelyn |title='Let them work': Hochul pressures Biden over New York's migrant surge |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/08/24/hochul-new-york-migrant-surge-00112835 |website=Politico |date=August 24, 2023 |access-date=25 August 2023}}</ref>

On February 12, 2025, U.S. Attorney General [[Pam Bondi]] announced that the [[United States Department of Justice|Department of Justice]] was suing Hochul and other New York officials, such as [[Letitia James]], over their handling of immigration issues.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.newsweek.com/bondi-ny-immigration-lawsuit-letitia-james-hochul-2030311|title=Pam Bondi Announces Lawsuit Against New York Over Immigration—'You're Next'|last1=Whisnant|first1=Greg|last2=Gooding|first2=Dan|publisher=[[Newsweek]]|date=February 12, 2025|access-date=February 12, 2025}}</ref> On April 16, 2026, the Associated Press reported that New York would lose nearly $73 million for refusing to revoke over 33,000 commercial driver's licenses flagged by the Department of Transportation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=español |first=JOSH FUNK Leer en |date=2026-04-16 |title=New York loses nearly $74 million for not revoking 33,000 illegal licenses for immigrant truckers |url=https://apnews.com/article/commercial-drivers-license-immigrants-new-york-6503c056455aa9cba114a1a2d646fedc |access-date=2026-06-03 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>

===Trade===
On September 17, 2011, [[Robert J. McCarthy]] noted that Hochul and her election opponent [[Jack Davis (industrialist)|Jack Davis]] both opposed [[free trade]]. "We saw what happened with NAFTA; the promises never materialized", Hochul said of the [[North American Free Trade Agreement]]. "If I have to stand up to my own party on this, I'm willing to do so."<ref>{{cite news|title=Hochul, in visit with Davis, voices anti-free trade stance|url= https://buffalonews.com/2011/09/17/hochul-in-visit-with-davis-voices-anti-free-trade-stance/ |last=McCarthy |first=Robert J. |date=September 17, 2011 |newspaper=The Buffalo News. buffalonews.com |access-date=September 10, 2011}}</ref> She opposed new free trade agreements then under consideration, saying, "We don't need to look any further than Western New York to see that these policies do not work." She believes that free trade agreements such as NAFTA and [[CAFTA]] have suppressed U.S. wages and benefits and caused job loss in the United States.

Hochul has vocally opposed [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|tariffs imposed during Donald Trump's second term]], especially those directed at Canada.<ref>{{cite news|title=Hochul warns Trump's tariffs, spending cuts could have ripple effect on N.Y.|url= https://spectrumlocalnews.com/nys/central-ny/politics/2025/03/05/hochul-warns-of-ripple-effect-trump-s-tariffs--spending-cuts-could-have-on-n-y- |last=Lisa |first=Kate |date=March 4, 2025 |newspaper=Spectrum News. spectrumlocalnews.com |access-date=November 22, 2025}}</ref> She said the tariffs amount to a 21% tax on imported goods and cost New York families $4,200 a year while reducing Canadian tourism by 400,000 visitors from 2024 to 2025.<ref>{{cite news|title=Tariffs act as 21% tax, cost NY families $4,200 per year, says Hochul
|url= https://www.wcax.com/2025/11/07/tariffs-act-21-tax-cost-ny-families-4200-per-year-says-hochul/ |date=November 7, 2025 |newspaper=WCAX 3. wcax.com |access-date=November 22, 2025}}</ref> In April 2025, New York and 11 other states sued the federal government to block the tariffs.<ref>{{cite news|title=12 states sue Trump, administration to block tariffs
|url= https://stateline.org/2025/04/24/12-states-sue-trump-administration-to-block-tariffs/ |last=Baumhardt |first=Alex |date=April 24, 2025 |newspaper=Stateline. stateline.org |access-date=November 22, 2025}}</ref> On June 16, Hochul and other Northeastern governors convened with five Canadian premiers in [[Boston]] to discuss the impact of Trump's tariffs on their economies.<ref>{{cite news|title=Eastern premiers look to U.S. governors as allies in Trump trade war
|url= https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/toronto/premiers-governors-trump-tariffs-boston-meeting-1.7560291 |last=Crawley |first=Mike |date=June 16, 2025 |newspaper=CBC News. cbc.ca/news |access-date=November 22, 2025}}</ref> Hochul stressed the need for U.S. governors and Canadian premiers to work together and forge new alliances against Trump's tariffs, to ensure economic growth for both countries.<ref>{{cite news|title=Scott, Hochul take part in discussions on Canadian tariffs
|url= https://www.wcax.com/2025/06/17/scott-hochul-take-part-discussions-canadian-tariffs/ |date=June 17, 2025 |newspaper=WCAX 3. wcax.com |access-date=November 22, 2025}}</ref>

On June 29, 2026, Hochul announced a $30 million state relief program aimed at insulating New York farmers from the effects of federal tariffs.<ref>{{cite news|title=Hochul launches $30M agricultural relief program targeting tariff-driven impacts
|url= https://www.wbng.com/2026/06/29/hochul-launches-30m-agricultural-relief-program-targeting-tariff-driven-impacts/|last=Jenkins |first=Jolie |date=June 29, 2026 |newspaper=WBNG 12. wbng.com |access-date=June 30, 2026}}</ref>

=== Guns ===
The ''Niagara Frontier'' reported that, during her time as County Clerk, gun rights advocates noted Hochul's expedited approval for gun permits. She was endorsed by the [[NRA Political Victory Fund]] (NRA-PVF) with an "A" rating in 2012. Her stance has evolved since then and New York has been called a leader on gun control by Everytown for Gun Safety.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gun violence in New York |url=https://www.everytown.org/state/new-york/ |access-date=2026-06-03 |website=Everytown |language=en-us}}</ref> Hochul signed legislation in 2022 that raised the age for buying a semiautomatic rifle and prohibited the carrying of handguns in many public spaces in response to the [[New York State Rifle & Pistol Ass'n v. Bruen|Bruen Decision]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Khan |first=Maysoon |date=2022-06-06 |title=NY governor signs law raising age to own semiautomatic rifle |url=https://apnews.com/article/kathy-hochul-new-york-gun-violence-government-and-politics-shootings-2af4ea8d1ed084d712de16db47b2bd6e |access-date=2026-06-03 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title=Guns and Gun Control |url=https://www.nytimes.com/topic/subject/guns-and-gun-control |access-date=2026-06-04 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Most of the law was upheld in 2023, when a federal court ruled New York may ban guns in parks, schools, and other areas public officials deem high-risk.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bromwich |first=Jonah E. |date=2023-12-08 |title=New York's Ban on Guns in Parks and Other 'Sensitive' Sites Is Affirmed |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/08/nyregion/new-york-gun-law.html |access-date=2026-06-04 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Hochul's critics have accused New York of Soviet-style gun control, especially with regard to [[3D printing]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Tuccille |first=J. D. |date=2026-02-25 |title=Politicians consider Soviet-style controls on 3D printers |url=https://reason.com/2026/02/25/politicians-consider-soviet-style-controls-on-3d-printers/ |access-date=2026-06-04 |website=Reason.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Personal life==
[[File:22nd September 11th Anniversary Remembrance Ceremony at Ground Zero in New York City on 11 September 2023 35.jpg|thumb|Kathy Hochul with her husband [[William J. Hochul Jr.]], Vice President [[Kamala Harris]], and Mayor [[Eric Adams]] in September 2023]]
Hochul is married to [[William J. Hochul Jr.]], the former [[United States Attorney for the Western District of New York]],<ref name=dem_and_chron_20110320/> who is also the senior vice president, general counsel, and secretary to [[Delaware North]], a hospitality and gambling company. They have two children named William and Caitlin.<ref>[https://www.yahoo.com/lifestyle/kathy-hochul-husband-bill-relationship-034612390.html Who Is Kathy Hochul's Husband, Bill & What Is Their Relationship History?] February 16, 2025 [https://web.archive.org/web/20250217214623/https://www.yahoo.com/lifestyle/kathy-hochul-husband-bill-relationship-034612390.html Archived] Yahoo.com</ref><ref name=dem_and_chron_20110320/> Hochul is Catholic.<ref>{{cite web|title=Churchill: 'Repulsive' abortion ruling is a win for Hochul's church|url=https://www.timesunion.com/churchill/article/Churchill-For-Hochul-and-other-Catholic-17268265.php|first=Chris|last=Churchill|date=June 28, 2022|access-date=July 3, 2022|publisher=[[Times Union (Albany)]]}}</ref>

Hochul is a founder of Kathleen Mary House, a transitional home for women and children who are victims of domestic violence.<ref name=kmh/> She has served on the organization's board. She also co-founded the Village Action Coalition, and, as of 2011, was a member of the board of trustees of [[Immaculata Academy (Hamburg, New York)|Immaculata Academy]] in [[Hamburg, New York]].

In 2024, Hochul was diagnosed with a [[basal-cell carcinoma]], a common form of skin cancer, on her nose.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wilkinson |first=Joseph |date=2024-09-12 |title=Gov. Hochul announces skin cancer diagnosis, surgical procedure |url=https://www.nydailynews.com/2024/09/12/kathy-hochul-skin-cancer-diagnosis/?lctg=C44554AE642785D2A4D974D7D7 |access-date=2024-09-12 |website=New York Daily News |language=en-US}}</ref> She underwent surgery to remove it in the same year.<ref name="gainer2024">{{Cite web |last=Gainer |first=Alice |date=September 13, 2024 |title=Gov. Kathy Hochul has basal cell carcinoma removed from nose. What to know about the common skin cancer - CBS New York |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/newyork/news/basal-cell-carcinoma-what-to-know/ |access-date=2025-11-07 |website=www.cbsnews.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

